{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"patriotic-abdicative-arrowana","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Problem\nThe Gerrit LLM-Review comment (`review_bot/adapter.py::_translate_findings`) and three CLI\ntext renderers (`_cli/_llm_commands.py`: plan-review advisory findings, code-review findings,\ncompletion-verdict findings) print the `severity` label to the human reviewer. Per the\nepic's ADR decision, the human-facing signal becomes blocking/advisory only (no raw priority\nnumber, no severity label). `_KERNEL_TO_COMMON_SEVERITY` is duplicated byte-for-byte in\n`code_review/shim.py` and `review_bot/adapter.py`; both copies exist solely to feed the\nlabel this ticket removes.\n\n## Approach\n1. In `review_bot/adapter.py::_translate_findings`, replace the `severity` key in each\n   translated finding dict with a `blocking: bool` (a finding present in `verdict[\"blocking\"]`\n   vs `verdict[\"advisory\"]`). Delete `_KERNEL_TO_COMMON_SEVERITY` from `adapter.py`.\n2. Delete `_KERNEL_TO_COMMON_SEVERITY` from `code_review/shim.py`'s `_to_common_finding`\n   likewise -- the common-finding shape now carries `priority`/`decision` from the prior\n   ticket, not a mapped severity label.\n3. Update the Gerrit comment body builder (`render_findings_block`/`_summarize`) to show\n   `[BLOCKING]`/`[advisory]` per finding instead of an uppercased severity word.\n4. Update the three CLI renderers in `_llm_commands.py` (`_render_plan_review_text`,\n   `_render_review_text`, `_render_verdict_text`) to print `BLOCKING`/`ADVISORY` (derived from\n   `decision`/`blocking` on the finding) instead of `f.get('severity')`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `grep -rn \"_KERNEL_TO_COMMON_SEVERITY\" src/` returns no matches.\n- [ ] The rendered Gerrit comment body for a sample verdict shows `BLOCKING` or `advisory` per\n  finding and never a severity word (critical/major/minor/high/medium/low/info).\n- [ ] `pytest tests/unit/test_review_bot.py -k translate_findings` and the adapter's\n  comment-rendering tests pass with updated assertions on the new tag text.\n- [ ] The `_llm_commands.py` renderer test module (locate via\n  `pytest --collect-only -q -k llm_commands`) passes with updated assertions for\n  `_render_plan_review_text`, `_render_review_text`, and `_render_verdict_text`.\n\n## Testing\nTier: in-process unit tests against the real rendering functions, fed a production-shaped\nverdict dict (contract test). Oracle: the exact rendered string contains `BLOCKING`/`advisory`\ntokens and none of the retired severity words. Refactoring litmus: renaming an internal\nhelper inside the renderer must not break these tests, since they assert on the printed\ntext, not on which helper produced it.","id":"96f9-e324-c22f-49a2","parent_id":"c4c8-317a-85ca-4b08","priority":2,"repos":["rebar"],"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Surface blocking/advisory instead of severity in review-bot and CLI renderers"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788112404358274736,"uuid":"23f74b54-6840-4fc2-9f96-8076a5dc9ce4"}